`), each with `/bin/sh -c` from the repository (at most 10 minutes per command): 1. **Base run** (`base_check`): every focused and regression command in a temporary detached worktree at the item\'s base (after any preparatory fix), with only this item\'s test files copied in. A test ID new at this step must not pass there: it must fail because the behaviour it asserts is missing, or fail to load the code this item adds (a guard that only checks or prints a file\'s path is not RED). An earlier step\'s cases already pass on a later step\'s base: they are counted for the floor, not judged (`earlier_steps` in the record). A command a platform card lists under Remote tests is not run on the base. A new test that already passes (a characterisation test, or a case committed before a send-back) goes in the result\'s `red_na: [{"id", "reason"}]`, which may name only a test ID new at this step (`tests/<action>-base<N>.md`). 2. **Candidate run** (`loop_run`): every recorded command (focused, regression, check) in the checkout. Each must exit 0, show every listed ID and run at least its floor (`tests/<action>-verify<N>.md`). The refusal prints each failing command, why it did not pass and the end of its output. A focused or regression command\'s **floor** is the larger of its base count and the most any earlier ShipLoop run of this action ran, less each test the result\'s `retired_tests: [{"id", "reason"}]` names that the base run showed. A test removed or newly skipped to pass is refused; a test this item deliberately retires is declared in `retired_tests`. A command passes only when it exits 0 **and ran tests** that ShipLoop can count and that show every listed ID. `shiploop red-check --run-dir <run> --action <test action> [--result <draft>]` runs the base check without completing. A draft result\'s `red_na` and `retired_tests` apply. It never gates and never counts toward the refused runs: exit 0 when no new test ID passes on the base, 1 when one does or a test did not run, 